class Solution {
public:
    int largestRectangleArea(vector<int>& heights) {
        int n = heights.size();
        vector<int> left(n), right(n);
        stack<int> st;

        // Nearest Smaller to Left
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            while (!st.empty() && heights[st.top()] >= heights[i]) st.pop();
            left[i] = st.empty() ? -1 : st.top();
            st.push(i);
        }

        while (!st.empty()) st.pop();

        // Nearest Smaller to Right
        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            while (!st.empty() && heights[st.top()] >= heights[i]) st.pop();
            right[i] = st.empty() ? n : st.top();
            st.push(i);
        }

        int maxArea = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int width = right[i] - left[i] - 1;
            maxArea = max(maxArea, heights[i] * width);
        }
        return maxArea;
    }
};
Approach — Largest Rectangle in Histogram

The main idea is to calculate, for every bar, how far it can extend to the left and right while maintaining at least its height.

For each heights[i]:

Find the Nearest Smaller Element to Left (NSL) → stored in left[i]
Find the Nearest Smaller Element to Right (NSR) → stored in right[i]
Then calculate the maximum rectangle using heights[i] as the minimum height.
1. Nearest Smaller to Left
while (!st.empty() && heights[st.top()] >= heights[i])
    st.pop();

left[i] = st.empty() ? -1 : st.top();
st.push(i);

The stack stores indices of bars in increasing height order.

left[i] tells us the first smaller bar on the left.

2. Nearest Smaller to Right

Same concept, but traverse from right to left:

while (!st.empty() && heights[st.top()] >= heights[i])
    st.pop();

right[i] = st.empty() ? n : st.top();
st.push(i);

right[i] tells us the first smaller bar on the right.

3. Calculate Width

For bar i:

width = right[i] - left[i] - 1;

Why -1?

Because left[i] and right[i] themselves are smaller bars and cannot be included.

So:

Width = Right Smaller Index - Left Smaller Index - 1
4. Calculate Area
area = heights[i] * width;

Then keep the maximum:

maxArea = max(maxArea, heights[i] * width);
Example

For:

heights = [2, 1, 5, 6, 2, 3]

For the bar 5:

Left smaller  = index 1
Right smaller = index 4

Therefore:

width = 4 - 1 - 1 = 2
area  = 5 × 2 = 10

So the maximum rectangle is 10.

Time Complexity
O(n)

There are 3 main passes:

Find smaller element on left → O(n)
Find smaller element on right → O(n)
Calculate areas → O(n)

Although there are while loops inside the for loops, each index is pushed and popped from the stack at most once per pass.

Therefore:

O(n) + O(n) + O(n) = O(n)
Space Complexity
O(n)

Because we use:

left → O(n)
right → O(n)
stack → O(n)
